DreamBank is a large, publicly available corpus of dream reports collected and curated by dream researchers. It was created by psychologist G. William Domhoff and colleagues as part of the scientific study of dreams.
📖 What DreamBank Contains

    25,000+ dream reports from individuals across different ages, genders, and cultures.

    Narratives are drawn from personal journals, laboratory studies, and long‑term collections.

    Each report is a raw text entry — a person describing their dream in their own words.

    Many reports are annotated using the Hall–Van de Castle coding system, which categorizes dream content (characters, emotions, interactions, settings).

🧠 Why It Matters

    Provides a massive dataset for studying dream content, meaning, and motifs.

    Enables semantic analysis (recurring symbols, archetypes, narrative structures).

    Supports cross‑cultural comparison of dream themes.

    Forms the backbone of modern empirical dream research, but it’s also useful for metaphysical and proto‑physics interpretations when treated as symbolic text.

